    {
      "id": "adr-exceptions",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Exceptions with a small domain hierarchy",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 0,
      "concern": "error-handling-logging",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code in this element fails: a dependency times out, input is wrong, a record is gone, a bug is hit. The forces: a failure that is swallowed becomes a wrong answer nobody can explain; one that is logged at every layer on its way up becomes five lines about one event; a caller that is handed a stack trace learns the file layout, the framework and sometimes the SQL; a log line without the request's id cannot be found when it matters; logs are read widely and copied to places with weaker access, so a password or an e-mail address in them is a leak; expected failures (not found, rejected, conflict) and unexpected ones (bugs, outages) need different handling, and a style that does not separate them handles both badly; and every convention here is kept by hundreds of hands, so it holds only if a tool checks it. What is done with the lines once written (shipping, retention, alerting, traces) is the Observability concern's decision, not this one's; that pack takes the shape and redaction decided here as given. The snippets assume one repository layout: source in src/ with the edge in src/http, the inner code in src/domain and src/application, the error types in src/errors, the logger in src/logger.js, and tests in test/; the TypeScript snippets name the Python, JVM, Go and .NET equivalents.",
      "decision": "Expected failures are thrown as subclasses of one base class {{BASE_ERROR}} in src/errors (NotFoundError, ValidationError, ConflictError, UpstreamUnavailableError, each with a stable code and an HTTP status hint; under ten classes in all); unexpected errors (TypeError, NullPointerException, driver errors nobody translated) are not caught below the boundary. Every wrap passes the original as the cause (Error.cause, inner exception, raise … from). Errors are handled by: {{BOUNDARY}}; the handler maps {{BASE_ERROR}} subclasses by their code, treats everything else as 500 and logs each failure once at the level its meaning asks for ({{LEVELS}}). A caller sees {{CALLER}}. Log lines are {{LOGS}} with the {{CORRELATION_FIELD}} from the logger's context (AsyncLocalStorage, MDC, contextvars), and {{REDACTED_FIELDS}} are kept out by {{REDACTION}} in the logger (pino redact or serializers, structlog processors, Serilog destructuring policies, logback masking). Conventions are held by the linters: ESLint (no-empty, preserve-caught-error, only-throw-error), Semgrep (log-and-continue, log-and-rethrow, whole objects), Ruff (BLE, TRY, LOG), Error Prone and Sonar on the JVM; in .NET the Roslyn analyzers CA1031 (do not catch general exceptions) and CA2200 (rethrow to preserve the stack), configured as errors in .editorconfig.",
      "consequences": "• Little ceremony: the happy path reads straight, and the frameworks already map exceptions to responses.\n• A function's signature does not say what it can fail with; reviewers and the hierarchy carry that knowledge.\n• An exception used for an expected case that is frequent (a cache miss, a validation on every field) costs stack capture; batch validation is reported as a notification object, not thrown field by field.\n• The hierarchy must stay small (under ten classes, checked) or it becomes a second type system nobody keeps up.",
      "alternatives": "1. Result types: failures in the signature, at the cost of handling at every call and fighting libraries that throw.\n2. Error codes from a catalogue: stable for clients, but codes compared by hand everywhere inside the code.\n3. Catch-all handlers per controller: the same mapping written many times, drifting apart.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "STYLE": [
          "exceptions"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-result",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Result types for expected failures",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 130,
      "concern": "error-handling-logging",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code in this element fails: a dependency times out, input is wrong, a record is gone, a bug is hit. The forces: a failure that is swallowed becomes a wrong answer nobody can explain; one that is logged at every layer on its way up becomes five lines about one event; a caller that is handed a stack trace learns the file layout, the framework and sometimes the SQL; a log line without the request's id cannot be found when it matters; logs are read widely and copied to places with weaker access, so a password or an e-mail address in them is a leak; expected failures (not found, rejected, conflict) and unexpected ones (bugs, outages) need different handling, and a style that does not separate them handles both badly; and every convention here is kept by hundreds of hands, so it holds only if a tool checks it. What is done with the lines once written (shipping, retention, alerting, traces) is the Observability concern's decision, not this one's; that pack takes the shape and redaction decided here as given. The snippets assume one repository layout: source in src/ with the edge in src/http, the inner code in src/domain and src/application, the error types in src/errors, the logger in src/logger.js, and tests in test/; the TypeScript snippets name the Python, JVM, Go and .NET equivalents.",
      "decision": "A function that can fail in an expected way returns a Result: Result<T, E> and the ? operator in Rust (thiserror for error types, anyhow only at the binary's edge), error values with %w and errors.Is/As in Go, kotlin.Result or Arrow's Either in Kotlin, neverthrow (or fp-ts Either) in TypeScript. The error type of each module names its cases and carries a stable code; wraps keep the source. Exceptions and panics are for bugs and broken invariants only and are never caught except at the boundary. An ignored Result fails the build (unused_must_use, errcheck, neverthrow/must-use-result). Errors are handled by: {{BOUNDARY}}; the handler maps the error cases to responses and logs each failure once at the level its meaning asks for ({{LEVELS}}). A caller sees {{CALLER}}. Log lines are {{LOGS}} with the {{CORRELATION_FIELD}} from the logger's context, and {{REDACTED_FIELDS}} are kept out by {{REDACTION}} in the logger (zap or slog handlers, tracing layers, pino redact or serializers).",
      "consequences": "• Every expected failure is in the signature; forgetting one is a compile error, not an incident.\n• Every call that can fail is handled or passed on explicitly, which is more code and a style the whole team must share.\n• Libraries that throw (HTTP clients, ORMs, JSON parsers) are wrapped at the module boundary into Results, and that wrapping is where bugs hide.\n• Unexpected errors still need the boundary handler, so the element has two mechanisms, not one.",
      "alternatives": "1. Exceptions with a hierarchy: less ceremony, failures invisible in signatures.\n2. Error codes from a catalogue: values too, but untyped; a Result's error type can carry the catalogue code.\n3. Results everywhere, bugs included: panics wrapped as values hide what should crash loudly.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "STYLE": [
          "result"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-codes",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Error codes from a catalogue",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 260,
      "concern": "error-handling-logging",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code in this element fails: a dependency times out, input is wrong, a record is gone, a bug is hit. The forces: a failure that is swallowed becomes a wrong answer nobody can explain; one that is logged at every layer on its way up becomes five lines about one event; a caller that is handed a stack trace learns the file layout, the framework and sometimes the SQL; a log line without the request's id cannot be found when it matters; logs are read widely and copied to places with weaker access, so a password or an e-mail address in them is a leak; expected failures (not found, rejected, conflict) and unexpected ones (bugs, outages) need different handling, and a style that does not separate them handles both badly; and every convention here is kept by hundreds of hands, so it holds only if a tool checks it. What is done with the lines once written (shipping, retention, alerting, traces) is the Observability concern's decision, not this one's; that pack takes the shape and redaction decided here as given. The snippets assume one repository layout: source in src/ with the edge in src/http, the inner code in src/domain and src/application, the error types in src/errors, the logger in src/logger.js, and tests in test/; the TypeScript snippets name the Python, JVM, Go and .NET equivalents.",
      "decision": "One catalogue in the repository (src/errors/catalogue.ts, or a generated enum from it) defines every error: a stable upper-case code (ORDER_NOT_FOUND), the HTTP status or exit status, a message template with named placeholders and a documentation URI. Code signals an error by returning or raising an error value built from a catalogue entry (fail('ORDER_NOT_FOUND', { orderId }) in TypeScript or Python, an error struct with the code in Go, an enum in C), never an ad-hoc string; a code that is not in the catalogue fails a test, and a renamed or removed code fails the catalogue snapshot (codes are added or deprecated, never changed). Errors are handled by: {{BOUNDARY}}; the handler maps the catalogue entry to the status and to {{CALLER}}, with the code as the stable member, and logs each failure once at the level its meaning asks for ({{LEVELS}}), with the code as a field. Log lines are {{LOGS}} with the {{CORRELATION_FIELD}} from the logger's context, and {{REDACTED_FIELDS}} are kept out by {{REDACTION}} in the logger. Unexpected errors map to one catalogue entry (INTERNAL) and keep their cause in the log only.",
      "consequences": "• Clients and support share one documented list; a code never changes meaning.\n• The catalogue is a shared file everyone edits; ownership and review of it must be clear or it fills with near-duplicates.\n• Codes are compared as values inside the code too, which is weaker than types; a generated enum limits typos.\n• Message templates in one place are easy to translate and easy to leave stale.",
      "alternatives": "1. Exceptions with a hierarchy: the type is the code; works until a client needs the list.\n2. Result types with an error enum per module: typed, but there is no one list across the element.\n3. Codes invented at call sites: what a catalogue replaces; nobody can list them.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "STYLE": [
          "codes"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-edge",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Errors handled once at the edge",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 390,
      "concern": "error-handling-logging",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code in this element fails: a dependency times out, input is wrong, a record is gone, a bug is hit. The forces: a failure that is swallowed becomes a wrong answer nobody can explain; one that is logged at every layer on its way up becomes five lines about one event; a caller that is handed a stack trace learns the file layout, the framework and sometimes the SQL; a log line without the request's id cannot be found when it matters; logs are read widely and copied to places with weaker access, so a password or an e-mail address in them is a leak; expected failures (not found, rejected, conflict) and unexpected ones (bugs, outages) need different handling, and a style that does not separate them handles both badly; and every convention here is kept by hundreds of hands, so it holds only if a tool checks it. What is done with the lines once written (shipping, retention, alerting, traces) is the Observability concern's decision, not this one's; that pack takes the shape and redaction decided here as given. The snippets assume one repository layout: source in src/ with the edge in src/http, the inner code in src/domain and src/application, the error types in src/errors, the logger in src/logger.js, and tests in test/; the TypeScript snippets name the Python, JVM, Go and .NET equivalents.",
      "decision": "One error handler per kind of edge owns the mapping from errors to the outside: an Express or Fastify error handler, a NestJS exception filter, a Spring @ControllerAdvice, an ASP.NET exception handler middleware, a FastAPI exception handler; a queue consumer's and a scheduled job's outer loop do the same for their failures (acknowledge, retry or dead-letter by error kind). The handler maps the error to {{CALLER}}, logs it exactly once with the cause chain and the {{CORRELATION_FIELD}}, and never leaks a stack trace in production. Domain and application code import no HTTP framework and know no status code; a dependency rule (dependency-cruiser, ArchUnit, import-linter) keeps it so.",
      "consequences": "• One place to get the mapping right, one log line per failure, and inner code reusable behind another edge.\n• The handler needs the error's kind to map it, so the error style must carry it (a subclass, a Result case or a code).\n• A library's raw error that reaches the handler untranslated becomes a 500, which is right for a bug and wrong for an expected case someone forgot to type.",
      "alternatives": "1. Translation per layer: keeps driver types out of the domain, at the cost of error classes per layer.\n2. Try/catch in every controller action: the same mapping written many times.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BOUNDARY": [
          "edge"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-layered",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Errors translated layer by layer",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 520,
      "concern": "error-handling-logging",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code in this element fails: a dependency times out, input is wrong, a record is gone, a bug is hit. The forces: a failure that is swallowed becomes a wrong answer nobody can explain; one that is logged at every layer on its way up becomes five lines about one event; a caller that is handed a stack trace learns the file layout, the framework and sometimes the SQL; a log line without the request's id cannot be found when it matters; logs are read widely and copied to places with weaker access, so a password or an e-mail address in them is a leak; expected failures (not found, rejected, conflict) and unexpected ones (bugs, outages) need different handling, and a style that does not separate them handles both badly; and every convention here is kept by hundreds of hands, so it holds only if a tool checks it. What is done with the lines once written (shipping, retention, alerting, traces) is the Observability concern's decision, not this one's; that pack takes the shape and redaction decided here as given. The snippets assume one repository layout: source in src/ with the edge in src/http, the inner code in src/domain and src/application, the error types in src/errors, the logger in src/logger.js, and tests in test/; the TypeScript snippets name the Python, JVM, Go and .NET equivalents.",
      "decision": "Each layer defines its own error types and translates the ones from below at its boundary: the persistence adapter catches driver errors (SQLException, DataAccessException, pg errors) and raises persistence errors (DuplicateKey, Unavailable); the domain and application layers raise their own (OrderNotFound, OrderAlreadyPaid), all extending {{BASE_ERROR}}, and wrap persistence errors they cannot handle; the API adapter maps domain errors to {{CALLER}} and logs each failure once with the cause chain and the {{CORRELATION_FIELD}}. Every translation keeps the original as the cause. An architecture test (ArchUnit, dependency-cruiser, import-linter) fails when the domain or application layer depends on a driver or framework error type, and the linters fail a translation that drops the cause.",
      "consequences": "• The domain depends on no vendor's types and the same application layer serves HTTP, messaging and batch edges.\n• More error classes and more code at each boundary; a lazy translation that drops the cause is the common bug, so the linters check for it.\n• Logging still happens once, at the edge; a layer that logs what it translates doubles the lines.",
      "alternatives": "1. Handled once at the edge: less code, but the domain sees driver errors unless it never catches at all.\n2. One flat hierarchy shared by all layers: fewer classes, but the domain imports the persistence errors.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BOUNDARY": [
          "layered"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-eslint-errors",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "TypeScript error rules",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 0,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 violations of no-empty (catch included), preserve-caught-error, only-throw-error, no-floating-promises and no-console in src on every pull request",
      "description": "ESLint (9.35 or later for preserve-caught-error) runs as a required check: an empty catch, a rethrow that drops the cause, a thrown non-Error, an unhandled rejected promise and a console call each fail the build. For .NET the matching Roslyn analyzers are CA1031 and CA2200 as errors in .editorconfig.\n\nTool: ESLint with typescript-eslint; Roslyn analyzers for .NET\n\n```js\n// eslint.config.js\nimport tseslint from 'typescript-eslint'\nexport default tseslint.config({\n  files: ['src/**/*.ts'],\n  languageOptions: { parserOptions: { projectService: true } },\n  rules: {\n    'no-empty': ['error', { allowEmptyCatch: false }],   // no swallowed errors\n    'preserve-caught-error': 'error',                     // new Error(msg, { cause }) when rethrowing (ESLint 9.35+)\n    '@typescript-eslint/only-throw-error': 'error',       // throw Error instances only (no-throw-literal, typed)\n    '@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises': 'error',   // a dropped rejection is a swallowed error\n    'no-console': 'error',                                // the logger, never console\n  },\n})\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-ruff-errors",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Python error rules",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 130,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 violations of the BLE, TRY, LOG, G, T20, S110 and B904 rules in src on every pull request",
      "description": "Ruff runs as a required check with the exception and logging rule families: a blind except that does not re-raise (BLE001, which covers a catch that only logs), a try-except-pass, a raise inside except without from, logging.error instead of logging.exception, an f-string in a log call and a print each fail the build.\n\nTool: Ruff\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[tool.ruff.lint]\nextend-select = [\n  \"BLE\",   # BLE001: blind except Exception without re-raise (a catch that only logs included)\n  \"TRY\",   # TRY002 raise bare Exception, TRY003 message built inline, TRY201 \"raise e\" where a bare raise re-raises,\n           # TRY203 useless try/except, TRY300 else clause, TRY400 logging.exception, TRY401 redundant exception in message\n  \"LOG\",   # logging module misuse, e.g. LOG015 root logger calls\n  \"G\",     # G004: f-string in a log call; data goes in extra=, not the message\n  \"T20\",   # T201 print, T203 pprint: the logger, never print\n  \"S110\",  # try-except-pass\n  \"B904\",  # raise ... from err inside except: keep the cause\n]\n[tool.ruff.lint.per-file-ignores]\n\"tests/**\" = [\"T20\", \"BLE001\"]\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-java-errors",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Java error rules",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 260,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 Error Prone errors from the exception patterns at compile time; 0 open Sonar issues of S108, S1166, S2139, S1181, S112, S106 on new code",
      "description": "Error Prone runs in the compiler with its exception patterns raised to errors; the SonarQube quality profile sets the matching rules to Blocker so the quality gate on new code fails on an empty catch, a dropped cause, log-and-rethrow, a caught Throwable, a thrown generic exception or System.out.\n\nTool: Error Prone (javac plugin) and SonarQube\n\n```xml\n<!-- pom.xml: maven-compiler-plugin -->\n<compilerArgs>\n  <arg>-XDcompilePolicy=simple</arg>\n  <arg>--should-stop=ifError=FLOW</arg>\n  <arg>-Xplugin:ErrorProne\n    -Xep:EmptyCatch:ERROR\n    -Xep:UnusedException:ERROR\n    -Xep:CatchAndPrintStackTrace:ERROR\n    -Xep:SystemOut:ERROR\n    -Xep:Finally:ERROR</arg>\n</compilerArgs>\n<!-- SonarQube quality profile, severity Blocker:\n     S108  empty block           S1166 exception handlers should preserve the original exception\n     S2139 logged and rethrown   S1181 Throwable and Error should not be caught\n     S112  generic exception thrown   S106 System.out used to log -->\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-semgrep-logging",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "No console, print, whole objects, log-and-continue or log-and-rethrow",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 390,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 Semgrep findings of the five logging rules in src on every pull request",
      "description": "Semgrep runs on src with five rules: console in TypeScript, print in Python, a log call passed a whole request, response, headers or body object (as a bare argument or as a property of the first object), a catch whose body is a single log call (the error is swallowed), and a catch that logs and rethrows (the error would be logged twice).\n\nTool: Semgrep\n\n```yaml\n# .semgrep/logging.yml   (semgrep --config .semgrep/ --error src/)\nrules:\n  - id: no-console\n    languages: [typescript, javascript]\n    severity: ERROR\n    message: Use the logger; console lines have no level, no correlation id and no redaction\n    pattern: console.$M(...)\n  - id: no-print\n    languages: [python]\n    severity: ERROR\n    message: Use the logger, not print\n    pattern: print(...)\n  - id: no-whole-object-logged\n    languages: [typescript, javascript, python]\n    severity: ERROR\n    message: Never log a whole request, response, headers or body; log the fields the serializer allows\n    patterns:\n      - pattern-either:\n          - pattern: '$LOG.$LEVEL(..., $OBJ, ...)'\n          - pattern: '$LOG.$LEVEL({ ..., $OBJ: $V, ... }, ...)'\n      - metavariable-regex:\n          metavariable: $LEVEL\n          regex: '^(error|warn|warning|info|debug|exception)$'\n      - metavariable-regex:\n          metavariable: $OBJ\n          regex: '^(req|request|res|response|ctx|event|body|headers)$'\n  - id: log-and-continue\n    languages: [typescript, javascript]\n    severity: ERROR\n    message: A catch that only logs swallows the error; rethrow it, return it, or handle it with a stated recovery\n    pattern: |\n      try { ... } catch ($E) { $LOG.$LEVEL(...); }\n  - id: log-and-rethrow\n    languages: [typescript, javascript]\n    severity: ERROR\n    message: Log once, where the error is handled; a catch that logs and rethrows logs it twice\n    pattern: |\n      try { ... } catch ($E) { ... $LOG.$LEVEL(...); ... throw ...; }\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-redact-denylist",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Secret paths redacted in the logger",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 520,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "Every path of {{REDACTED_FIELDS}} is in the logger's redact list; a unit test logging each of them at the top level and one level down sees only the redaction marker",
      "description": "The logger is built once by a factory with a redact list of the known secret and personal-data paths and level labels as words; the exported logger and the test's logger come from the same factory, so the test logs an object holding each path at the top level and one level down into a stream and asserts that the written line holds only the marker. structlog: a processor that walks event_dict for the same keys; Serilog: Destructurama.Attributed or a masking enricher with the same list.\n\nTool: pino (redact) with a Vitest test; structlog processors for Python\n\n```js\n// src/logger.js\nimport pino from 'pino'\nexport const PATHS = '{{REDACTED_FIELDS}}'.split(/,\\s*/)\nexport const createLogger = (stream) => pino({\n  level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL ?? 'info',\n  formatters: { level: (label) => ({ level: label }) },   // \"level\":\"error\", not pino's number\n  redact: { paths: [...PATHS, ...PATHS.map((p) => `*.${p}`), 'req.headers.authorization', 'req.headers.cookie'], censor: '[REDACTED]' },\n}, stream)\nexport const logger = createLogger(pino.destination(1))\n\n// test/logger.test.js\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\nimport { PATHS, createLogger } from '../src/logger.js'\nit('redacts every listed path at the top level and one level down', () => {\n  const lines = []\n  const log = createLogger({ write: (l) => lines.push(l) })\n  log.info({ ...Object.fromEntries(PATHS.map((p) => [p, 'secret-1'])), user: Object.fromEntries(PATHS.map((p) => [p, 'secret-2'])) }, 'sign-in')\n  expect(lines[0]).not.toMatch(/secret-[12]/)\n  expect((lines[0].match(/\\[REDACTED\\]/g) ?? []).length).toBe(PATHS.length * 2)\n})\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REDACTION": [
          "denylist"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-redact-allowlist",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Only allowed fields leave the logger",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 650,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "100 % of top-level log fields are on the allow-list and every object field goes through a serializer; a unit test logging an unknown field and a whole user object sees neither",
      "description": "The logger's formatter drops every top-level field that is not on the allow-list, and each object type has a serializer that picks its allowed fields, so nothing is logged whole; the exported logger and the test's logger come from the same factory, and the test logs an unknown field and a whole user record into a stream and asserts that only the allowed fields are written. structlog: a processor that filters event_dict to the same list.\n\nTool: pino (formatters and serializers) with a Vitest test; structlog processors for Python\n\n```js\n// src/logger.js\nimport pino from 'pino'\nconst ALLOWED = new Set(['level', 'time', 'msg', 'service', '{{CORRELATION_FIELD}}', 'err', 'req', 'user', 'order', 'durationMs', 'code', 'status'])\nconst pick = (keys) => (o) => Object.fromEntries(keys.filter((k) => o?.[k] !== undefined).map((k) => [k, o[k]]))\nexport const createLogger = (stream) => pino({\n  base: { service: 'order-api' },\n  formatters: {\n    level: (label) => ({ level: label }),                                               // \"level\":\"error\", not pino's number\n    log: (o) => Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(o).filter(([k]) => ALLOWED.has(k))),\n  },\n  serializers: {\n    req: (r) => ({ id: r.id, method: r.method, path: String(r.url).split('?')[0] }),   // no headers, no body\n    user: pick(['id', 'role']),                                                           // never name, email, phone\n    order: pick(['id', 'status', 'total']),\n    err: pino.stdSerializers.err,\n  },\n}, stream)\nexport const logger = createLogger(pino.destination(1))\n\n// test/logger.test.js\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\nimport { createLogger } from '../src/logger.js'\nit('writes only allowed fields, never a whole object', () => {\n  const lines = []\n  createLogger({ write: (l) => lines.push(l) }).info({ user: { id: 7, email: 'a@b.c' }, password: 'x' }, 'sign-in')\n  expect(lines[0]).toContain('\"user\":{\"id\":7')\n  expect(lines[0]).not.toMatch(/a@b\\.c|password/)\n})\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REDACTION": [
          "allowlist"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-log-shape",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Every log line well-formed, correlated and levelled",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 780,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "100 % of the lines written during a full test run parse as {{LOGS}}, carry a level from {{LEVELS}}, a {{CORRELATION_FIELD}} of 16 to 36 hex characters and a message of at most {{MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS}} characters; 0 JSON lines with a 4xx status at level error",
      "description": "The test run writes its logs to a file; a test afterwards reads every line and asserts the shape: JSON lines must parse, hold the fields, carry a level word from the list and never pair a 4xx status with level error; text lines must match the fixed layout built from the same levels and limit. One malformed, uncorrelated or mislevelled line fails the build.\n\nTool: Vitest (a test over the test run's log file); the same test under pytest for Python\n\n```ts\n// test/log-shape.test.ts  (the app writes LOG_FILE=test-logs/app.log during the run)\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\nconst FIELD = '{{CORRELATION_FIELD}}'\nconst LEVELS = '{{LEVELS}}'.split(/,\\s*/)\nconst MAX = {{MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS}}\nconst ID = /^[0-9a-f-]{16,36}$/   // a trace id, or a UUID with dashes\nconst TEXT = new RegExp(`^\\\\d{4}-\\\\d\\\\d-\\\\d\\\\dT\\\\S+ (${LEVELS.map((l) => l.toUpperCase()).join('|')}) \\\\[[0-9a-f-]{16,36}\\\\] .{1,${MAX}}(\\\\s\\\\w+=\\\\S+)*$`)\nit('every log line of the test run is well-formed, correlated and levelled by meaning', () => {\n  const lines = readFileSync('test-logs/app.log', 'utf8').split('\\n').filter((l) => l && !l.startsWith(' '))   // indented = stack trace\n  expect(lines.length).toBeGreaterThan(0)\n  for (const line of lines) {\n    if (line.startsWith('{')) {\n      const o = JSON.parse(line)\n      expect(LEVELS, line).toContain(o.level)\n      expect(o[FIELD], line).toMatch(ID)\n      expect(String(o.msg ?? o.message).length, line).toBeLessThanOrEqual(MAX)\n      if (typeof o.status === 'number' && o.status >= 400 && o.status < 500) expect(o.level, `a 4xx logged at error: ${line}`).not.toBe('error')\n    } else expect(line).toMatch(TEXT)\n  }\n})\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-no-stack-to-caller",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "A 500 carries no stack trace",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 910,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "0 stack-trace patterns, class names, paths or SQL in the body of a 500 response with production settings; the body holds exactly the agreed fields and the {{CORRELATION_FIELD}}",
      "description": "An integration test starts the element with production settings, calls a route that throws an unexpected error, and asserts the response shape ({{CALLER}}) and that the body matches no stack-trace, class-name, path or SQL pattern. The same test with a route that throws an expected error checks the mapped status.\n\nTool: Vitest with supertest; the same test with httpx under pytest for Python\n\n```ts\n// test/http/error-response.test.ts   (NODE_ENV=production in this file's setup)\nimport request from 'supertest'\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\nimport { app } from '../../src/app'   // registers GET /__boom in tests only: throws new TypeError('kaput')\n\nconst LEAKS = /\\n\\s+at |TypeError|kaput|\\/src\\/|node_modules|\\.ts:\\d+|SELECT |INSERT |localhost|10\\.\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+/\n\nit('an unexpected error reaches the caller as a clean 500', async () => {\n  const res = await request(app).get('/__boom').set('traceparent', '00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01')\n  expect(res.status).toBe(500)\n  expect(JSON.stringify(res.body)).not.toMatch(LEAKS)\n  expect(res.body['{{CORRELATION_FIELD}}']).toBe('4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736')\n  expect(Object.keys(res.body).sort()).toMatchSnapshot()   // the agreed fields, nothing more; reviewed when it changes\n  // problem details: expect(res.headers['content-type']).toMatch(/application\\/problem\\+json/); expect(res.body.type).toMatch(/^https?:/)\n})\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-error-hierarchy",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Error classes extend the base and stay few",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1040,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "Every class exported from src/errors extends {{BASE_ERROR}} and carries a non-empty string code; at most 10 classes",
      "description": "A test imports every export of src/errors, instantiates each class and asserts that it is an instance of {{BASE_ERROR}} and of Error, that its code is a non-empty string, and that the module exports at most ten classes; only-throw-error in the lint keeps thrown values to Error instances. On the JVM the same is an ArchUnit rule over classes ending in Exception; in Python a test over the subclasses of the base.\n\nTool: Vitest (a test over src/errors); ArchUnit for the JVM\n\n```ts\n// test/errors/hierarchy.test.ts\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\nimport * as errors from '../../src/errors'\nimport { {{BASE_ERROR}} } from '../../src/errors'\n\nconst classes = Object.entries(errors).filter(([, v]) => typeof v === 'function' && v.prototype instanceof Error && v !== {{BASE_ERROR}}) as Array<[string, new (message?: string) => Error & { code?: unknown }]>\n\nit('exports at most ten error classes', () => {\n  expect(classes.length, classes.map(([n]) => n).join(', ')).toBeLessThanOrEqual(10)\n})\nit.each(classes)('%s extends {{BASE_ERROR}} and carries a code', (name, Cls) => {\n  const e = new Cls('x')\n  expect(e, `${name} does not extend {{BASE_ERROR}}`).toBeInstanceOf({{BASE_ERROR}})\n  expect(typeof e.code === 'string' && e.code.length > 0, `${name} has no stable code`).toBe(true)\n})\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "STYLE": [
          "exceptions"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-edge-imports",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Only the edge maps errors to responses",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1170,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 modules outside src/http import the error handler or the response mapping; 0 domain or application modules import an HTTP framework",
      "description": "A dependency rule fails the build when any module outside the edge imports the error-to-response mapping, or when domain or application code imports the HTTP framework (and so could build a response or know a status code). On the JVM the same rule is an ArchUnit test; in Python, import-linter.\n\nTool: dependency-cruiser; ArchUnit for the JVM; import-linter for Python\n\n```js\n// .dependency-cruiser.cjs\nmodule.exports = {\n  forbidden: [\n    {\n      name: 'only-the-edge-maps-errors',\n      comment: 'ADR Errors handled once at the edge: only src/http knows the response shape and status codes',\n      severity: 'error',\n      from: { path: '^src/', pathNot: '^src/http/' },\n      to: { path: '^src/http/(error-handler|problem)\\\\.ts$' },\n    },\n    {\n      name: 'inner-code-knows-no-http',\n      comment: 'domain and application code throws or returns; it does not build responses',\n      severity: 'error',\n      from: { path: '^src/(domain|application)/' },\n      to: { path: 'node_modules/(express|fastify|@nestjs/common|hono|koa)' },\n    },\n  ],\n}\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BOUNDARY": [
          "edge"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-layer-errors",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Driver errors stay below the domain",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1300,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 dependencies from the domain or application packages on driver, persistence or HTTP-client exception types; 0 public persistence-adapter methods declaring a driver exception; every domain exception extends {{BASE_ERROR}}",
      "description": "An ArchUnit test fails the build when a domain or application class depends on a driver or framework exception type, when a persistence adapter method declares a driver exception, or when a domain exception does not extend {{BASE_ERROR}}. In TypeScript the same rules are dependency-cruiser rules on the driver packages (pg, mysql2, mongodb, undici) from src/domain and src/application.\n\nTool: ArchUnit (JUnit 5); dependency-cruiser for TypeScript\n\n```java\n// src/test/java/com/shop/orders/LayerErrorsTest.java\n@AnalyzeClasses(packages = \"com.shop.orders\")\nclass LayerErrorsTest {\n  @ArchTest\n  static final ArchRule domain_sees_no_driver_errors = noClasses()\n      .that().resideInAnyPackage(\"..domain..\", \"..application..\")\n      .should().dependOnClassesThat().resideInAnyPackage(\n          \"java.sql..\", \"org.springframework.dao..\", \"jakarta.persistence..\", \"org.springframework.web.client..\");\n\n  @ArchTest\n  static final ArchRule adapters_translate_driver_errors = methods()\n      .that().areDeclaredInClassesThat().resideInAPackage(\"..adapter.persistence..\").and().arePublic()\n      .should().notDeclareThrowableOfType(java.sql.SQLException.class);\n\n  @ArchTest\n  static final ArchRule domain_errors_extend_the_base = classes()\n      .that().resideInAPackage(\"..domain..\").and().haveSimpleNameEndingWith(\"Exception\")\n      .should().beAssignableTo({{BASE_ERROR}}.class);\n}\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BOUNDARY": [
          "layered"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-result-must-use",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "No Result ignored",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1430,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 Results left unhandled in src: the build fails on an unused Result, an unchecked error value or an unwrap outside tests",
      "description": "The linter of the language makes a dropped Result a build error: neverthrow/must-use-result in TypeScript, unused_must_use plus clippy's unwrap_used and expect_used in Rust, errcheck, errorlint and wrapcheck in golangci-lint (v2 configuration).\n\nTool: eslint-plugin-neverthrow (TypeScript); clippy (Rust); golangci-lint (Go)\n\n```js\n// eslint.config.js (added for the result style)\nimport neverthrow from 'eslint-plugin-neverthrow'\nexport default [{\n  files: ['src/**/*.ts'],\n  plugins: { neverthrow },\n  rules: { 'neverthrow/must-use-result': 'error' },   // a Result never matched or unwrapped is a dropped error\n}]\n\n// Rust, src/lib.rs:  #![deny(unused_must_use, clippy::unwrap_used, clippy::expect_used)]   (allowed under #[cfg(test)])\n// Go, .golangci.yml (v2):\n//   version: \"2\"\n//   linters:\n//     enable: [errcheck, errorlint, wrapcheck]\n//     settings: { errcheck: { check-blank: true, check-type-assertions: true } }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "STYLE": [
          "result"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-codes-catalogue",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Every code is in the catalogue and stable",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1560,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "100 % of codes used in src exist in the catalogue; the sorted list of codes matches its snapshot, so a rename or removal fails the build",
      "description": "A test collects every code used in src, checks each against the catalogue, snapshots the sorted list of codes so that a removed or renamed code is a reviewed change, and checks that every entry has a status, a documentation URI and a message template under the length limit.\n\nTool: Vitest (a test over the error catalogue); the same test under pytest for Python\n\n```ts\n// test/errors/catalogue.test.ts\nimport { execSync } from 'node:child_process'\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\nimport { CATALOGUE } from '../../src/errors/catalogue'   // { ORDER_NOT_FOUND: { status: 404, type: 'https://…/errors/order-not-found', message: 'Order {orderId} not found' }, … }\n\nconst used = new Set(execSync(\"grep -rhoE \\\"fail\\\\('[A-Z_]+'\\\" src | grep -oE '[A-Z_]+'\").toString().trim().split('\\n'))\n\nit('every code used in src is in the catalogue', () => {\n  for (const code of used) expect(CATALOGUE, `${code} is not in the catalogue`).toHaveProperty(code)\n})\nit('codes are added or deprecated, never renamed or removed', () => {\n  expect(Object.keys(CATALOGUE).sort()).toMatchSnapshot()\n})\nit('every entry has a status, a documentation URI and a short message', () => {\n  for (const [code, e] of Object.entries(CATALOGUE)) {\n    expect(e.status, code).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(400)\n    expect(e.type, code).toMatch(/^https?:\\/\\//)\n    expect(e.message.length, code).toBeLessThanOrEqual({{MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS}})\n  }\n})\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "STYLE": [
          "codes"
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
